ach of the three possibilities one checks directly (by listing the six points of \\(S_3\\)) that the two sunny lines would have to contain two points with the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate or two points with the same \\(y\\)‑coordinate, which would make one of them parallel to a forbidden direction – a contradiction. Hence \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\n   Consequently for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\nCombining the constructions and the impossibility for \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\) yields the complete description stated in the verdict.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\)\n\n*If a line is parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\)*, it is \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(b\\le n+1-c\\), so at most \\(n\\) points lie on it.  \nThe same argument works for lines parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\) and to \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).\n\n*If a line is sunny*, two distinct points on it cannot have the same\n\\(x\\)‑coordinate (otherwise the line would be parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\)),\nnor the same \\(y\\)‑coordinate (otherwise it would be parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\)).\nHence a sunny line meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point,\nso it contains at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) possible columns;\nconsequently it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+1-\\frac{i-1}{i}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n\n*Border points.*  \nFor \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\) the point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn+1-a = \\frac{a-1}{a}\\,a+1-\\frac{a-1}{a},\n\\]\n\nhence \\((a,n+1-a)\\in L_a\\).  Consequently every point of the\nborder \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) lies on a distinct line \\(L_a\\).\n\n*Interior points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Choose the integer\n\\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le a\\) such that \\(b = n+1-c-(a-c)\\); then\n\\((c,n+1-c)\\) is a border point belonging to \\(L_c\\) and the two points\n\\((c,n+1-c)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfy the equation of \\(L_c\\).  Hence\n\\((a,b)\\in L_c\\).  Thus every interior point also belongs to one of the\nlines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so \\(k=n\\) is possible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The case \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny.  For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y=\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,x+1-\\frac{j}{j+1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAgain the slope \\(\\frac{j}{j+1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); each\n\\(M_j\\) is sunny.  The line \\(M_j\\) contains the two points  \n\n\\[\n(j+1,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(j+1,j),\n\\]\n\nthe latter belonging to the border because \\((j+1)+j=n+1\\).\nHence column \\(x=j+1\\) is completely covered by \\(M_j\\).  \nAll points with \\(x=1\\) are covered by \\(V\\).  Consequently the \\(n\\) lines\n\\(\\{V,M_1,\\dots ,M_{n-1}\\}\\) satisfy the required condition and contain\nexactly one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(v=n-k\\;( \\ge 2)\\).  \nUse the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor each remaining column \\(a=v+1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nN_a:\\; y=\\frac{a-1}{a}\\,x+1-\\frac{a-1}{a}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{a-1}{a}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(N_a\\) is sunny.\nThe line \\(N_a\\) contains the two points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\);\nthe latter lies on the border, the former is the lowest point of column\n\\(a\\).  Because the column‑size equals \\(n+1-a\\le k\\), the single sunny line\n\\(N_a\\) covers the whole column \\(a\\).\n\nThus the family consisting of the \\(v\\) vertical lines (3) and the\n\\(k=n-v\\) sunny lines (4) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers\nevery point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe constructions of Sections 2–5 give configurations with \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that a configuration with exactly two sunny\nlines exists.  The third line must be non‑sunny; without loss of\ngenerality suppose it is vertical, say \\(x=1\\).  Then the points\n\\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) remain uncovered.  Any sunny line can contain at\nmost one point with \\(x=2\\) and at most one point with \\(x=3\\); therefore\nthe two sunny lines together can cover at most three of the four remaining\npoints, but one of the points \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) has the same sum\n\\(a+b=4\\).  A sunny line cannot contain two points with the same sum,\notherwise it would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  Consequently the four points\ncannot be covered by two sunny lines, a contradiction.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is\nimpossible.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------