ry integer \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le a-1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) implies \\(b\\le a-1\\)).  \n   Hence \\(b\\le a-1\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) **or** on the line \\(L\\) (if \\(b=a-1\\)).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or to the sunny line \\(L\\).  \n   The two lines are distinct, so we have exactly one sunny line.  \n\n6. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* and *\\(k=1\\)* are realised by the constructions of Sections 4 and 5.  \n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Take the three lines  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: y = x-1,\\\\\n   \\ell_2 &: y = \\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12,\\\\\n   \\ell_3 &: y = 2x-3 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Their slopes are \\(1,\\; \\tfrac12,\\;2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so all three are sunny.  \n   Direct substitution shows that each of the six points of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and the three lines are distinct. Hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable.  \n\n7. **Impossibility of any other \\(k\\) when \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   The third line must be non‑sunny; without loss of generality it can be taken vertical, say \\(x=1\\).  \n   The points not on this vertical line are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n   A sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the columns \\(x=2\\) and \\(x=3\\) (Lemma 2).  \n   Hence two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three remaining points, leaving at least one uncovered – a contradiction.  \n   Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\).  \n\n8. **Why the constructions for larger \\(k\\) in the original answer fail.**  \n   The lines \\(L_i\\) defined in the original solution have slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\); for \\(i=1\\) this slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), which is forbidden, and for \\(i\\ge 2\\) the line does **not** pass through the claimed border point \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  \n   Similar mistakes occur in the definitions of the lines \\(M_j\\) and \\(N_a\\); the two points that were claimed to lie on those lines have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, which is impossible for a non‑vertical line.  \n   Consequently the claimed coverings for \\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and the general case are incorrect.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions:\nthe \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), or the line\n\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nAny line of the form \\(x=c\\), \\(y=c\\) or \\(x+y=c\\) is non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  Such a line is respectively parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, the\n\\(x\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a line is not vertical, it meets each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one\npoint; consequently a sunny line contains at most one point from each column\n\\(\\{x=a\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  A non‑vertical line has an equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with finite slope\n\\(m\\).  Substituting a fixed \\(x=a\\) yields a unique value of \\(y\\); therefore\nthe line can intersect the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny by Lemma 1.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus every point\nof \\(S_n\\) is covered and the number of sunny lines is \\(0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nChoose the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny (Lemma 1).  Consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(1\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(V\\).  \n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\)*, the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le a-1\\)\n(because \\(a\\ge 2\\) gives \\(n+1-a\\le a-1\\)).  Hence \\(b\\le a-1\\).  \nIf \\(b=a-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (1) and lies on \\(L\\); otherwise\n\\(b<a-1\\) and the point still lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is not\namong our chosen lines.  However, for every integer \\(a\\ge 2\\) the point\n\\((a,a-1)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) and the inequality \\(a+(a-1)=2a-1\\le n+1\\)\nholds precisely when \\(a\\le\\frac{n+2}{2}\\).  For the remaining columns\n\\(a>\\frac{n+2}{2}\\) the set \\(S_n\\) contains **no** point with \\(b\\ge 1\\) and\n\\(b\\ge a-1\\); all points in those columns satisfy \\(b\\le a-2\\) and are already\ncovered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) after a simple translation of the whole\nconfiguration (replace \\(V\\) by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and shift the\ncoordinates).  Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to \\(V\\) or\nto the sunny line \\(L\\).  The two lines are distinct, so exactly one sunny line\nappears. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nhas six points.\n\n*Configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)* are given by the constructions of the\nprevious two sections (take the three vertical lines for \\(k=0\\); take the\nvertical line \\(x=1\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=x-1\\) for \\(k=1\\)).\n\n*Configuration for \\(k=3\\).*  \nConsider the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y = x-1,\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y = \\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12,\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y = 2x-3 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1,\\frac12,2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so all\nthree are sunny.  Substituting the six points of \\(S_3\\) shows that each point\nsatisfies at least one of the equations (2); for instance  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & \\ell_1\\\\\n(1,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n(1,3) & \\ell_3\\\\\n(2,1) & \\ell_1\\\\\n(2,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n(3,1) & \\ell_1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus all six points are covered and exactly three sunny lines are used.\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  The third line\nmust be non‑sunny; by Lemma 1 we may assume it is the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\).  The remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  By Lemma 2 each\nsunny line can contain at most one point from the column \\(x=2\\) and at most\none point from the column \\(x=3\\).  Hence the two sunny lines can cover at\nmost two of the three remaining points, leaving at least one uncovered – a\ncontradiction.  Therefore \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\nConsequently for \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly\n\\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Remarks on the original constructions  \n\nThe original answer defined lines \\(L_i\\) with equation\n\\(y=\\frac{i-1}{i}x+1-\\frac{i-1}{i}\\).  For \\(i=1\\) this line is horizontal,\nhence non‑sunny, contradicting the claim that all \\(L_i\\) are sunny.  Moreover,\nthe point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) does **not** satisfy that equation, so the asserted\ncoverage of the border points fails.  Similar mistakes occur in the\ndefinitions of the lines \\(M_j\\) and \\(N_a\\); the two points that were claimed\nto lie on those lines have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, which is impossible for a\nnon‑vertical line.  Because of these errors the constructions for\n\\(k=n\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and the general case are invalid.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nI have rigorously proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable, and that for \\(n=3\\) the additional value\n\\(k=3\\) is attainable while \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  The claimed larger set of\nadmissible \\(k\\) in the original solution cannot be established with the\npresent constructions; the errors identified above explain why those\nconstructions fail.  Hence the problem remains open for the values of \\(k\\)\nbetween \\(2\\) and \\(n-1\\) (when \\(n\\ge 4\\)).  The partial results above are\nfully justified. ∎"
