ed.  \n   But column\u202f2 actually contains \\(n-1\\) points, so covering all of them would require **exactly** \\(n-1\\) of the non\u2011sunny lines to be horizontal or diagonal and each of them to meet column\u202f2 at a distinct required point, while the sunny line must meet column\u202f2 at the remaining point.  This forces every non\u2011sunny line to be of a different type (horizontal or diagonal) and to pass through a distinct lattice point of column\u202f2.  However, a horizontal line can pass through at most one lattice point of column\u202f2, and a diagonal line can also pass through at most one such point.  Since there are only two possible families (horizontal and diagonal) but we have \\(n-1\\ge 3\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the pigeon\u2011hole principle implies that at least two of the non\u2011sunny lines belong to the same family.  Two distinct horizontal (or two distinct diagonal) lines are parallel, hence they intersect column\u202f2 at the **same** \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate, contradicting the requirement that the points of column\u202f2 have distinct \\(y\\)\u2011coordinates.  \n   Therefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist when \\(n\\ge 4\\).  \n\n7. **Consequences.**  \n   * The value \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \n   * The value \\(k=3\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).  \n   * No configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for any \\(n\\ge 4\\).  \n\nThese are the only rigorous conclusions obtained; the determination of the remaining admissible values of \\(k\\) for general \\(n\\) is left open.  \n\n\u220e"

