emma 1 (Covering by vertical lines).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are non‑sunny and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.  \n  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always realizable.\n\n* **Lemma 2 (A single sunny line suffices for the last column).**  \n  For any \\(n\\ge3\\) the set\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{L\\},\n  \\qquad\n  L:\\;y=\\tfrac12x-\\tfrac12\n  \\]\n  consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(L\\)) is sunny, and together they cover \\(S_n\\).  \n  Indeed, the vertical lines cover all points with abscissa \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\); the only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\), and \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=\\tfrac12 n-\\tfrac12\\), so it lies on \\(L\\).  \n  Consequently \\(k=1\\) is always realizable.\n\n* **Lemma 3 (Two sunny lines cannot cover the remaining points).**  \n  Assume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  \n  Let the other \\(n-2\\) lines be non‑sunny; each of them belongs to \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\).  \n  Choose a column that is **not** covered by a vertical line (such a column exists because there are at most \\(n-2\\) vertical lines).  \n  In that column the number of points equals the column height \\(h\\ge2\\).  \n  A non‑sunny line that is not vertical meets the column in at most one point, and each sunny line also meets the column in at most one point (otherwise it would be vertical).  \n  Hence at most \\((n-2)+(2)=n\\) points of the column can be covered, but the column contains \\(h=n+1-a\\ge2\\) points and the total number of lines is only \\(n\\); a direct counting of incidences shows that at least one point of the column would have to be covered twice, contradicting the requirement that the lines be distinct.  \n  A more transparent contradiction is obtained by observing that the two sunny lines must each contain at least two points of the column (otherwise the column could not be completely covered because the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines contribute at most one point each).  \n  Two distinct points of the same column determine a vertical line, which is forbidden for a sunny line.  \n  Therefore no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n* **Consequences.**  \n  - \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable.  \n  - \\(k=2\\) is never attainable.  \n\nThe remaining values of \\(k\\) (in particular, which odd values \\(k\\ge3\\) are possible) are not settled by the present argument.  \n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
