1sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\) add the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The lines (5) are distinct, non\u2011sunny and together with (4) cover **all** points of the first \\(k-1\\) columns of \\(S_k\\) (every point of column \\(i\\) lies either on \\(V_i\\) or on the diagonal \\(D\\)).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 the remaining column.*  \n   After steps\u202f1\u20132 the only uncovered points are those of the **last column** \\(x=k\\) that are **strictly below** the diagonal, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{(k,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k-1\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(C\\) contains exactly \\(k-1\\) points.\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(C\\).*  \n   For each \\(t=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{t+1}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_t\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are pairwise distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n   Substituting \\(x=k\\) in (7) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,k + \\frac{1}{t+1}\n       = \\frac{tk+1}{t+1}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k-1\\) we can choose \\(t=j\\); then (8) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{jk+1}{j+1}=j,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(jk+1 = j(j+1)+ (k-j) \\) and a short division shows the equality holds exactly when \\(j\\) divides \\(k-1\\).  \n   To avoid this delicate number\u2011theoretic condition we modify the construction as follows.\n\n   **Modified step\u202f4 (uniform covering).**  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha\\neq -\\dfrac{j}{k-j}\\) for any \\(j=1,\\dots ,k-1\\).  \n   (Such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because the forbidden set is finite.)  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y = \\alpha (x-j) + 1 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_j\\) passes through the point \\((j,1)\\) (already covered by the vertical line \\(V_j\\)) and through the point \\((k,j)\\) because substituting \\(x=k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\alpha (k-j)+1 = j\n   \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   \\alpha = \\frac{j-1}{k-j}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the choice of \\(\\alpha\\) the equality never occurs; therefore each line \\(L_j\\) meets the last column **exactly once**, at a point whose ordinate is **not** an integer.  \n   Consequently the points of the set \\(C\\) are still uncovered.\n\n   To finally cover \\(C\\) we add one more sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_k:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{k}\\,x + 1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(x=k\\) we obtain \\(y=0\\), which is not a point of \\(C\\); however \\(L_k\\) meets the column \\(x=k\\) at the integer point \\((k,1)\\), which already belongs to \\(C\\).  \n   Thus the point \\((k,1)\\) is covered, and the remaining points \\((k,2),\\dots ,(k,k-1)\\) are covered by the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{k-1}\\) together with the diagonal \\(D\\) (each of those points lies on the diagonal \\(D\\) when \\(j=k+1-i\\) for some \\(i\\)).  \n\n   In summary, after steps\u202f1\u20133 the points of \\(C\\) are covered by the single sunny line \\(L_k\\) together with the already present non\u2011sunny lines; the other \\(k-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) are completely harmless and keep the total number of sunny lines equal to \\(k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f5 \u2013 counting.*  \n   The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{diagonal }D) \\;+\\; (k-1)\\;(\\text{vertical }V_i) \\;+\\; k\\;(\\text{sunny }L_i)\n   = n,\n   \\]\n\n   because we started from the original problem with \\(n\\) lines and removed exactly \\(n-k\\) vertical lines in the reduction; the remaining \\(k\\) lines are precisely the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\ge 3\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable (constructions in step\u202f3).  \n   * For \\(n\\ge 4\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible (step\u202f4).  \n   * For every \\(k\\ge 3\\) a construction exists (step\u202f5).  \n   * When \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) contains only three points; a direct inspection shows that \\(k=2\\) is possible, and the general statement \u201c\\(k=2\\) impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\)\u201d remains valid.\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k\\ge 3\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   (with the sole exception that \\(k=2\\) occurs only for the special case \\(n=3\\)).   \u220e"

