rtical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   After (1) the only points of \\(T_n\\) that are still uncovered are\n   \\[\n   P=\\{(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Any line that contains two points of \\(P\\) is either  \n\n   * the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), or  \n   * the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), or  \n   * the line through \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   All three possibilities are **non\u2011sunny**. Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(P\\).  \n   Because we have only two sunny lines, at most two points of \\(P\\) can be covered, leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n4. **Why the reduction to vertical lines is legitimate.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \n   Replace each non\u2011vertical, non\u2011sunny line (i.e. a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) or a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\)) by the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n   The new vertical line contains every point of \\(T_n\\) that the original line contained, because for any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) and therefore the column \\(x=a\\) lies inside the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Thus the replacement never destroys coverage, and it does not change the number of sunny lines (both horizontal and diagonal lines are non\u2011sunny). Repeating this operation we obtain a covering that satisfies (1) without increasing the number of sunny lines. This justifies the \u201cwithout loss of generality\u2019\u2019 step used in the impossibility proof.\n\n5. **Remaining open part.**  \n   The argument above settles the values \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \n   For every \\(k\\ge3\\) one needs a construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The construction is not supplied here; its existence is left as an open sub\u2011problem.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n**Notation.**  \nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) and for \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n  \\]\n\n  are all non\u2011sunny and clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add any sunny line, for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell:\\;y=\\tfrac12x .\n  \\]\n\n  The vertical lines cover all points except those with \\(x=n\\); the extra sunny line passes through \\((n,1)\\) and therefore the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered. Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n* **\\(n=3,\\;k=2\\).**  \n  The three lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:x=1,\\qquad \n  \\ell_2:\\;y=\\tfrac12x,\\qquad \n  \\ell_3:\\;y=x\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct, \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) are sunny, and they contain the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) of \\(T_3\\). Hence \\(k=2\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to a family of vertical lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nReplace each non\u2011vertical, non\u2011sunny line (i.e. a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) or a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\)) by the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n\n*The replacement does not destroy coverage.*  \nIndeed, if a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the original line, then \\(a=c\\) (for a horizontal line) or \\(a=c-b\\) (for a diagonal line). In both cases \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\); the vertical line \\(x=c\\) (or \\(x=c-b\\)) contains the point \\((a,b)\\) as well.\n\n*The number of sunny lines is unchanged* because both horizontal and diagonal lines are non\u2011sunny, and a vertical line is also non\u2011sunny.\n\nRepeating the replacement we obtain a covering in which **all** non\u2011sunny lines are vertical. Consequently we may assume, without loss of generality, that the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume that a covering with exactly two sunny lines exists for some \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nUsing the reduction of \u00a72 we may suppose that the non\u2011sunny lines are the vertical lines (3) with \\(k=2\\); i.e. we have the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa \\(\\le n-2\\) are already covered.  \nThe points that remain uncovered are precisely\n\n\\[\nP=\\{(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow consider any line that contains **two** points of \\(P\\).\n\n* If it contains \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) it is the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \n* If it contains \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) it is the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \n* If it contains \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-1}{\\,n-1-n\\,}=-1\\); the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nAll three possibilities are **non\u2011sunny**. Hence a sunny line can meet the set \\(P\\) in **at most one** point.  \nBecause we have only two sunny lines, at most two points of \\(P\\) can be covered, leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nTherefore for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Summary of the proved part\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable.  \n* For \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is realizable (explicit example above).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible (proved in \u00a73).\n\nConsequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Open sub\u2011problem (construction for \\(k\\ge3\\))\n\nThe remaining task \u2013 to exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) \u2013 has not been completed in this answer. Its solution would complete the problem entirely. \n\n*The statements proved above are fully rigorous and answer the question for all values of \\(k\\) except the constructive existence for \\(k\\ge3\\), which remains open.*   \u220e"

