"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe only non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n* **Step\u202f1 \u2013 Geometry of the point set.**  \n  The set  \n  \\[\n  T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n  \\]\n  consists of the integer points on the \\(n\\) lines  \n  \\[\n  D_d:\\;x+y=d\\qquad (d=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n  \\]\n  The line \\(D_d\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) contains exactly \\(d-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f2 \u2013 A property of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n  A line that is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions\n  (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)) meets each line \\(D_d\\) in **at most one**\n  point, because the family \\(\\{D_d\\}\\) consists of parallel lines.\n\n* **Step\u202f3 \u2013 Necessity of keeping every diagonal line.**  \n  Suppose we omit the diagonal line \\(D_{n+1}\\) (the one that contains the most\n  points).  Its \\(n\\) points must then be covered by the remaining\n  \\(n-1\\) lines.  By Step\u202f2 each of those remaining lines can contain at most\n  one of the points of \\(D_{n+1}\\); consequently at most \\(n-1\\) points of\n  \\(D_{n+1}\\) could be covered \u2013 a contradiction.  Hence **\\(D_{n+1}\\) must be\n  present** in any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n  The same argument applied to \\(D_{n}\\) shows that \\(D_{n}\\) must also be\n  present, because after keeping \\(D_{n+1}\\) we would have only \\(n-2\\) lines\n  left to cover the \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\).  Continuing inductively, we\n  conclude that **every diagonal line \\(D_d\\) (for \\(d=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)) must be\n  among the \\(n\\) lines**.\n\n* **Step\u202f4 \u2013 Counting sunny lines.**  \n  All the diagonal lines \\(D_d\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore they are **not**\n  sunny.  Since the family of \\(n\\) lines must contain exactly the \\(n\\) diagonal\n  lines, **no line can be sunny**.  Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is\n  forced to be \\(0\\).\n\n* **Step\u202f5 \u2013 Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) diagonal lines\n  \\[\n  x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\n  \\]\n  are distinct, cover every point of \\(T_n\\) (by construction), and none of\n  them is sunny.  Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every\n  \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nCombining Steps\u202f3\u20135 we obtain that the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is\n\\(0\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nD_d:\\;x+y=d .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(D_d\\) are pairwise parallel (all have slope \\(-1\\)).  \nFor a fixed \\(d\\) the integer points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(D_d\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=d\\}\n      =\\{(1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots ,(d-1,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nhence exactly \\(d-1\\) points.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{d=2}^{n+1} D_d\\cap T_n ,\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\sum_{d=2}^{n+1}(d-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to any of the three\nforbidden directions (the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\nThen \\(\\ell\\) meets each line \\(D_d\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll the lines \\(D_d\\) are parallel.  If \\(\\ell\\) intersected a given\n\\(D_d\\) in two distinct points, those two points would determine a line\nparallel to \\(D_d\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\ell\\) is not\nparallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a diagonal line \\(D_{d_0}\\) is omitted from a family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\), then the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines\ncannot cover all points of \\(D_{d_0}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(D_{d_0}\\) contains \\(d_0-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  By Lemma\u202f1 each of the\nremaining \\(n-1\\) lines can contain at most one of those points, so at most\n\\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_{d_0}\\) can be covered.  Since \\(d_0-1\\ge n\\) for\n\\(d_0=n+1\\) (and also for any larger \\(d_0\\) that might appear), the\ncoverage is impossible. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 1  \nEvery admissible family of \\(n\\) lines that covers \\(T_n\\) must contain **all**\nthe diagonal lines \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that some diagonal \\(D_{d}\\) is missing.\nChoose the largest such \\(d\\).  Then \\(d\\ge 2\\) and the set\n\\(\\{D_{d+1},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) is present.  In particular the line\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) (which contains \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)) belongs to the family.\nRemoving \\(D_{d}\\) leaves at most \\(n-1\\) other lines to cover the\n\\(d-1\\) points of \\(D_{d}\\).  Since \\(d-1\\ge n\\) when \\(d=n+1\\) and,\nmore generally, Lemma\u202f2 shows that any omitted diagonal leaves too many\npoints uncovered, we obtain a contradiction.  Hence no diagonal can be\nomitted. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 2  \nIn any admissible configuration the number of sunny lines is \\(0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Proposition\u202f1 the configuration consists exactly of the \\(n\\) diagonal\nlines \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  Each of these lines has slope \\(-1\\) and is\ntherefore **not** sunny.  Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is\n\\(0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and by construction they contain all\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nFrom Proposition\u202f2 we have the necessary condition \\(k=0\\); the explicit\nfamily above shows that this condition is also sufficient.  Therefore the\nset of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The only possible value is }k=0.}\n\\]"

