int of \\(T_n\\) whose\n   first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 3.2 – the remaining points.*  \n   The points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   R_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{3.2}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed diagonal \\(D_d\\) with \\(d\\ge n-k+2\\) the set\n   \\(R_k\\cap D_d\\) consists of the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,d-a)\\qquad a=n-k+1,\\;n-k+2,\\dots ,d-1 .\n   \\tag{3.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Its cardinality is \\(d-1-(n-k)\\le k\\).\n\n   *Step 3.3 – sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y = i\\,x + b_i ,\\qquad \n   b_i:=\\bigl(n-k+1+i\\bigr)- (1+i)\\bigl(n-k+i\\bigr).\n   \\tag{3.4}\n   \\]\n\n   *   The slope \\(i\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(s_i\\) is\n       sunny.\n   *   The lines are distinct because they have different slopes.\n   *   For any diagonal \\(D_d\\) (\\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\)) the intersection point of\n       \\(s_i\\) with \\(D_d\\) is obtained from  \n\n       \\[\n       x+(i x+b_i)=d\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n       x=\\frac{d-b_i}{1+i}.\n       \\tag{3.5}\n       \\]\n\n       Substituting the definition of \\(b_i\\) gives  \n\n       \\[\n       x=\\frac{d-\\bigl(n-k+1+i-(1+i)(n-k+i)\\bigr)}{1+i}\n         =\\frac{d-(n-k+1+i)}{1+i}+ (n-k+i)\n         = n-k+i+\\frac{d-(n-k+1+i)}{1+i}.\n       \\tag{3.6}\n       \\]\n\n       Since \\(d\\) and \\(i\\) are integers, the fraction in (3.6) is an integer\n       exactly when \\(d\\ge n-k+1+i\\); in that case  \n\n       \\[\n       x=n-k+i,\\;n-k+i+1,\\dots ,d-1 .\n       \\tag{3.7}\n       \\]\n\n       Consequently, for a fixed diagonal \\(D_d\\) the set of intersection\n       abscissae \\(\\{x_{i}(d)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) equals precisely the set\n       \\(\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,d-1\\}\\), i.e. the abscissae of all points of\n       \\(R_k\\cap D_d\\) (see (3.3)).  Hence every point of \\(R_k\\) lies on one\n       of the sunny lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\).\n\n4. **Verifi